<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gojong of Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gwangmu Emperor, Emperor Gojong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gojong was the 26th king of the Joseon dynasty from 1863 and the first emperor of the Korean Empire, which he proclaimed in 1897. His reign saw the forced opening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa">Treaty of Ganghwa</a>, the murder of Queen Min and growing Japanese domination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> forced him to abdicate in 1907, and his death in 1919 helped spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea.jpg" alt="Gojong of Korea" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This photo was taken in 1907. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gojong_of_the_Korean_Empire_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gojong was born into a distant branch of the royal House of Yi, a descendant of Grand Prince Inpyeong, the third son of King Injo. When King Cheoljong died without a son, the influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong-kim-clan">Andong Kim clan</a> nominated him as the next king, and he was crowned on 13 December 1863. Queen Sinjeong acted as regent, assisted by his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Heungseon Daewongun</a>, who remained the dominant figure at court. The Daewongun was the chief proponent of isolationism and persecuted native and foreign Catholics, a policy that led to French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1866 and 1871. Gojong announced his direct rule in 1873, and after the Daewongun's retirement in November 1874 Queen Min and the Yeoheung Min clan controlled the court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa">Treaty of Ganghwa</a> of 1876 was the first unequal treaty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> signed with a foreign country. It gave Japanese citizens extraterritorial rights and opened the ports of Busan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Chemulpo</a> (Incheon) and Wonsan to trade. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident">Imo Incident</a> of 1882, soldiers of the old army, unpaid for 13 months, rioted, and Queen Min asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> for help; about 3,000 Qing soldiers came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. The Kapsin Coup of December 1884 failed after three days. In 1895 Japanese agents, in a plot organised by the Japanese minister Miura Gorō, killed Queen Min in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>. In February 1896 Gojong fled to the Russian legation in Seoul and governed from there for about a year.</p><p>On 13 October 1897 he proclaimed himself emperor of the Great Korean Empire at the altar of Hwangudan, with the era name Gwangmu, a gesture marking the end of Qing suzerainty. The constitution of 17 August 1899 granted him absolute power. His reforms improved the military, industry and education to some degree, and in 1898 he abolished the Independence Club. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> lost its diplomatic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</a>. Gojong refused to sign it and appealed to other powers, including a letter to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>, but without result.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gojong was forced to abdicate on 20 July 1907, and his son Sunjong replaced him. He was confined to Deoksugung and made several attempts to escape, in 1910, 1915 and 1918, all unsuccessful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was formally annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on 22 August 1910, and Gojong received the title King Emeritus Yi of Deoksu. He died suddenly at Deoksugung on 21 January 1919, aged 66. The official cause was a cerebral haemorrhage, but suspicion that Japan had poisoned him arose around the time of his death and persists. His death and funeral were a catalyst for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>. He is buried with his wife at Hongneung in Namyangju, part of the Royal Tombs of the Joseon dynasty, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gojong of Korea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regent Heungseon Daewongun, father of King <b>Gojong</b>, strengthened the throne from 1864 and repelled French and American expeditions, but Japan forced the country open with the Treaty of Ganghwa in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Gojong</b> then wanted an anthem as a symbol of the state: the German musician Franz von Eckert, who had led the band of the German Navy and worked for many years in Japan, came to Korea in 1901 and completed the Korean Empire's national anthem, based on…</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukga">Aegukga</a></li><li><blockquote>After Japan's victory over Qing China in 1895 ended Korea's tributary ties, Japanese agents murdered Queen Min, and <b>King Gojong</b> took refuge in the Russian legation in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/state/korean-empire">Korean Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Unhyeongung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Deoksugung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.99&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Unhyeongung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7722 812 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gojong of Korea"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8172 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.99&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
